<!-- ─── §01 THE BOTTLENECK ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-bottleneck" data-code="bottleneck">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Bottleneck</div>
<h2>When Parallel Decoding Meets Sparse Experts</h2>

<p>Autoregressive language models generate one token at a time. Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures are a natural fit: each token routes to a small subset of experts (e.g., 2 out of 64), so only those expert weights need to be in memory. The router picks two weights, loads them, computes, done.</p>

<p><strong>Diffusion LLMs work differently.</strong> Instead of generating left-to-right, they start with a fully masked sequence and iteratively unmask tokens over multiple denoising steps. Crucially, each forward pass processes an entire <em>block</em> of tokens simultaneously — a block of 32 masked positions gets resolved in one go. This is the source of the speedup, but it creates a hidden problem when combined with MoE.</p>

<p>Each of those 32 tokens independently routes to its own 2 experts. In the worst case, all 32 tokens pick different pairs — that's 64 unique expert weights to load from memory for a single forward pass, when you only "needed" 2 per token. In practice on LLaDA2.0-mini, the average is around <strong>70 unique experts</strong> per forward pass. Loading all of those expert weights repeatedly is what makes MoE dLLM inference memory-bound.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">block-size vs unique experts loaded</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a block size to see the difference</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="k-btns" id="block-btns">
      <button class="k-btn active" data-block="1">Block = 1 token</button>
      <button class="k-btn" data-block="4">Block = 4 tokens</button>
      <button class="k-btn" data-block="8">Block = 8 tokens</button>
      <button class="k-btn" data-block="16">Block = 16 tokens</button>
    </div>
    <div style="margin-bottom:.75rem;font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted)">
      Expert pool (16 experts, each token picks 2). <span style="color:var(--purple-l)">Purple</span> = loaded this pass.
    </div>
    <div id="expert-pool-grid" style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px;margin-bottom:.75rem"></div>
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:.5rem">
      <div style="font-size:.8rem;color:var(--muted2)">Unique experts loaded: <strong id="unique-count" style="color:var(--purple-l)">2</strong> / 16</div>
      <div style="font-size:.8rem;color:var(--muted2)">Memory reads vs token-by-token: <strong id="mem-ratio" style="color:var(--amber)">1×</strong></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The empirical confirmation is stark: on LLaDA2.0-mini, MoE latency dominates end-to-end inference time (over 50% in both the Transformers and SGLang backends), and MoE kernel latency grows <em>linearly</em> with the number of unique experts activated per forward pass.</p>

<h3>Why existing MoE tricks don't transfer</h3>
<p>Standard MoE efficiency work — expert pruning, merging, skipping — was designed for autoregressive models where each forward pass processes one token. Some approaches exploit temporal consistency (reusing the same expert assignments across sequential token positions), but in a diffusion LLM the "temporal" dimension is the denoising step, not the sequence position. DES, a recent approach for dLLMs, applies a candidate-constraint strategy at inference time without additional training, achieving moderate compression but suffering severe accuracy collapse under aggressive compression (from 72% to 53.8% on MATH500 at comparable expert budgets).</p>
</section>

<!-- ─── §02 SCORES AS SIGNALS ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-scores" data-code="scores">
<div class="section-no">02 — Scores as Signals</div>
<h2>Router Weights Reveal Expert Importance</h2>

<p>Before designing a solution, dMoE identifies two key observations from empirical study on LLaDA2.0-mini with GSM8K data.</p>

<p><strong>Observation A:</strong> Token-level expert scores — the softmax logits output by the router — are positively correlated with how much an expert contributes to the output. Specifically, the authors sample 10<sup>8</sup> data points, mask out individual experts, measure the resulting increase in reconstruction loss, and compare against the router weights assigned to those experts.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">router weight vs loss increase (GSM8K, Pearson r = 0.46)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">each dot is one expert activation sample</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding:.75rem">
    <canvas id="scatter-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The correlation isn't perfect (r = 0.46) — some low-weight experts still turn out to be important, and some high-weight ones are redundant. But the trend is clear enough to use as a heuristic: if a token's router assigns a high weight to expert E5, E5 is probably doing real work. This motivates aggregating router weights across the block to find the experts that matter most collectively.</p>

<p><strong>Observation B:</strong> Expert concentration varies substantially across denoising steps and transformer layers. Early denoising steps (where most tokens are still masked) tend to spread activations broadly across the expert pool. Later steps (where most tokens are settled) show more concentrated patterns where a handful of experts dominate. Measured as coefficient of variation, unique expert count has CV ≈ 17% across benchmarks — a strong argument against using a fixed-size coreset.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Key insight</div>
  Block-level expert scores = <span class="math-var">Σ</span>(token-level expert scores)<br>
  <span class="math-comment">→ experts with high aggregate weight are important for the whole block</span><br><br>
  Coreset size = <span class="math-var">top-p</span>(block scores) — adapts dynamically<br>
  <span class="math-comment">→ concentrated blocks get small coresets; dispersed blocks get larger ones</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─── §03 THE dMoE ALGORITHM ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-algorithm" data-code="algorithm">
<div class="section-no">03 — The dMoE Algorithm</div>
<h2>Aggregate → Normalize → Select Coreset → Restrict Routing</h2>

<p>The dMoE algorithm processes one block of tokens through four stages. Use the navigator below to walk through each step with a concrete 4-token example (block of tokens "dLLMs", "have", "memory", "bounds" in a MoE layer with 8 experts, each token picking top-2).</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">dMoE routing — step-by-step</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="algo-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Token-Level Routing (standard MoE)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Each of the 4 tokens computes its router scores over all 8 experts. Each token picks its top-2.
            <br>"dLLMs" → &#123;E2, E5&#125; &nbsp; "have" → &#123;E5, E7&#125; &nbsp; "memory" → &#123;E1, E5&#125; &nbsp; "bounds" → &#123;E3, E8&#125;
            <br>Unique experts loaded: <strong style="color:var(--amber)">7 of 8</strong> — almost the whole pool!</div>
          <div class="step-code">si = Router(ti)  # shape [|E|]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Aggregate to Block-Level Scores</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Sum the token-level expert scores (after zeroing out non-top-k entries) across all tokens in the block. Experts that are important for many tokens get high aggregate weight.
            <br>S_block = Σ masked_top2(si) for each token i
            <br>E5 appeared in 3 tokens → high aggregate score; E3, E8 each appeared once → lower.</div>
          <div class="step-code">Sblock += TopKMask(si, k=2)  # accumulate</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Top-p Coreset Selection</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Normalize block scores to sum to 1. Apply top-p: greedily select experts by descending score until the cumulative probability exceeds p.
            <br>At p=0.6: select &#123;E5, E2, E1&#125; — 3 experts cover 60%+ of the block's weight.
            <br>Unique experts loaded: <strong style="color:var(--green)">3 of 8</strong> (was 7!).</div>
          <div class="step-code">C = TopP(normalize(Sblock), p=0.6)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Coreset-Restricted Token Routing</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Each token re-routes within the coreset only. Token-level expert count stays the same (still top-2 per token), but all tokens now draw from the shared coreset.
            <br>"dLLMs" → &#123;E2, E5&#125; &nbsp; "have" → &#123;E5, E1&#125; &nbsp; "memory" → &#123;E1, E5&#125; &nbsp; "bounds" → &#123;E5, E2&#125;
            <br>All tokens satisfied; only 3 unique experts ever loaded.</div>
          <div class="step-code">Ri = TopK(mask_outside(si, C), k=2)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="algo-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <span id="algo-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 4</span>
      <button id="algo-next">Next →</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Notice what doesn't change: each token still picks its top-k experts (k=2 in LLaDA2.0-mini's 8-expert setting). The key insight is that by restricting the pool to the coreset, you force all tokens to draw from the same small set — which is already the set of globally important experts for that block, so most tokens would have chosen from it anyway.</p>

<h3>Training: self-distillation with CSE loss</h3>
<p>To recover accuracy after restricting routing, dMoE is fine-tuned using self-distillation. The same routing procedure used at inference is applied during the forward pass. The loss is a Coreset Selection Evidence (CSE) loss that encourages the model to maintain its original output quality under the coreset constraint. Training uses ~700K samples generated by LLaDA2.0-mini itself from GSM8K, PRM12K, Numina-Math, and OpenThoughts — entirely self-supervised, no external curated data needed.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─── §04 ADAPTIVE TOP-P ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-topp" data-code="topp">
<div class="section-no">04 — Adaptive Top-p</div>
<h2>One Threshold, Different Coreset Sizes</h2>

<p>The elegant part of dMoE is that top-p selection is <em>adaptive</em>. A fixed coreset size (top-k experts) would be too aggressive when the block is diverse and too loose when the block is concentrated. Top-p naturally adapts: if the block's expert distribution is peaked (one or two experts dominate), those experts alone reach the probability threshold and the coreset is tiny. If the distribution is flat (many experts contribute equally), you need more of them to reach the threshold.</p>

<p>This matters because expert concentration varies substantially across denoising steps. In early steps, all tokens are still masked and the model hasn't committed to any structure — it spreads weight broadly. In later steps, most tokens have been unmasked and routing converges to a concentrated set. Top-p naturally handles both regimes with a single threshold p.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">top-p coreset selection — two different block distributions</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">switch p to see how coreset size adapts</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:.875rem">
      <div style="flex:1;min-width:200px">
        <div style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:.5rem">SCENARIO A — concentrated (late denoising step)</div>
        <canvas id="dist-canvas-a" style="width:100%;height:110px;display:block"></canvas>
        <div style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted2);margin-top:.4rem">Coreset: <span id="coreset-a" style="color:var(--cyan);font-weight:700">—</span> experts</div>
      </div>
      <div style="flex:1;min-width:200px">
        <div style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:.5rem">SCENARIO B — dispersed (early denoising step)</div>
        <canvas id="dist-canvas-b" style="width:100%;height:110px;display:block"></canvas>
        <div style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted2);margin-top:.4rem">Coreset: <span id="coreset-b" style="color:var(--cyan);font-weight:700">—</span> experts</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="k-btns" id="p-btns">
      <button class="k-btn" data-p="0.4">p = 0.4</button>
      <button class="k-btn active" data-p="0.6">p = 0.6</button>
      <button class="k-btn" data-p="0.7">p = 0.7</button>
      <button class="k-btn" data-p="0.8">p = 0.8</button>
    </div>
    <div style="font-size:.75rem;color:var(--muted);font-style:italic">With p=0.6 the concentrated block needs only 2 experts while the dispersed block needs 8 — the same threshold adapts automatically.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>At inference, the same <code>p</code> value used during training (p<sub>train</sub>) is the default, but it can be overridden. This makes dMoE <strong>tunable at runtime</strong> — operators can lower p to reduce expert loads and latency at the cost of a small accuracy dip, or raise p to recover more performance on hardware with available memory bandwidth. No re-training required.</p>
</section>

