<!-- ─── §06 TUNABLE TRADE-OFF ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-tradeoff" data-code="tradeoff">
<div class="section-no">06 — Tunable Trade-off</div>
<h2>Dial p at Runtime — No Retraining</h2>

<p>Unlike pruning or distillation approaches that bake a fixed compression ratio into the model, dMoE's coreset size is adjustable at inference time via the p threshold. Two model checkpoints were trained (p<sub>train</sub> = 0.6 and p<sub>train</sub> = 0.5); each can be evaluated at any p<sub>test</sub> ∈ &#123;0.4, 0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8&#125;.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">unique expert count vs MATH500 accuracy — varying p_test</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">two trained checkpoints shown as separate curves</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding:.75rem">
    <canvas id="tradeoff-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1.5rem;margin-top:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted2)"><div style="width:18px;height:3px;background:var(--purple);border-radius:2px"></div>p_train = 0.6</div>
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted2)"><div style="width:18px;height:3px;background:var(--cyan);border-radius:2px;border-top:2px dashed var(--cyan)"></div>p_train = 0.5</div>
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">◇ p_test = 0.4 &nbsp; ◈ p_test = 0.5 &nbsp; ● p_test = 0.6 &nbsp; ◆ p_test = 0.7 &nbsp; ■ p_test = 0.8</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Both checkpoints sweep a smooth Pareto frontier from high compression (p=0.4 → ~10 experts, ~70% MATH500) to moderate compression (p=0.8 → ~27 experts, ~74% MATH500). The original model uses 70 experts for 72% — dMoE at p=0.7 hits 72.6% with only 19 experts, strictly dominating the original on the efficiency frontier.</p>

<h3>Block size generalization</h3>
<p>A model trained with block size 32 generalizes to smaller blocks (8, 16, 24) without retraining. As the block size shrinks, there are fewer tokens to aggregate, so fewer unique experts were activated in the first place — and dMoE's coreset compression still reduces them further. Accuracy is maintained or slightly improved at smaller block sizes (e.g., MATH500 73.6% → 74.4% at block size 16), suggesting the technique is robust to the exact block size used during inference.</p>

<h3>Summary</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>4.77× reduction</strong> in unique activated experts (70 → 14.6 average)</li>
  <li><strong>99.11% performance retained</strong> at p=0.6 across MATH500, GSM8K, ARC-C, MMLU</li>
  <li><strong>76–80% memory</strong> reduction in MoE parameter footprint</li>
  <li><strong>1.14–1.66× end-to-end speedup</strong> measured on hardware (4× H100)</li>
  <li><strong>Tunable at runtime</strong> — p can be adjusted without retraining to match available memory budget</li>
</ul>
</section>

